  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Direct human absolute-bioavailability measurements in transferable small-molecule contexts are predominantly above 20%: imidafenacin 57.8%, tolvaptan 56%, ACT-1004-1239 53%, bromazepam 86%, and lenvatinib ~85%.",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C05", "C08", "C07", "C12"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The closest structural contexts to the query (benzamide/indazole and lipophilic benzamide) are high-F: venadaparib has good human oral bioavailability and 70.63% in mice, while tolvaptan is 56% and not affected by food, supporting the fasted condition.",
      "card_ids": ["C18", "C24", "C05", "C21"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "New L2 preclinical records for imidafenacin, lenvatinib, and venadaparib remain above 20% (36.1%, 78.4%, 70.63%), indicating the high-F pattern is not species-restricted.",
      "card_ids": ["C31", "C28", "C24"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The apatinib context is internally inconsistent (L1 'good' vs L2 10-20%) and the L2 value lacks species/dose/comparator detail; it is less structurally aligned than the high-F benzamide/indazole contexts and does not affirmatively establish F<20% for the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C04", "C22"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Ilomastat's poor oral bioavailability is a peptidomimetic hydroxamic acid context with low structural transfer to the query's non-peptidic thioether-indazole amide.",
      "card_ids": ["C16"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The physicochemical prior notes high logD and solubility risk, but this is not endpoint proof; high-F contexts include lipophilic poorly soluble drugs such as tolvaptan and lenvatinib.",
      "card_ids": ["C05", "C12"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "associated_nonvoting_records",
      "applicability": "high",
